<section class="content-section">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Converting HST to EST</h2>
  <div class="use-cases">
    <article>
      <h3>1. Assuming Hawaii Shifts Clocks</h3>
      <p>
        A frequent mistake is assuming Hawaii enters daylight saving time in summer. Hawaii stays at UTC-10 year-round, so the gap changes from 5 hours to 6 hours when mainland East Coast states switch to EDT.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>2. Date Boundary Oversights</h3>
      <p>
        When converting evening HST times (such as 7:00 PM to 11:00 PM HST), adding 5 hours moves the EST time past midnight into the next calendar day.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>3. Confusing HST with HST/HAST in Alaska</h3>
      <p>
        While the Aleutian Islands share UTC-10 in winter, Alaska observes DST, whereas Hawaii does not. Always check whether your destination is Hawaii or Alaska.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

